  "query_preview": "..\n\nA bit more clarity with the forecast, as the lead shortwave over the \nMidwest not as strong as forecasted yesterday and will result in a \nflatter wave tracking near or just south of the southern Michigan \nborder this afternoon/evening. Sufficient instability to support \nsevere storms is not expected to cross north of the border \n(supported by the machine learning). Although, it still is a close \nenough call for SPC to maintain the marginal risk for Lenawee and \nparts of Washtenaw/Monroe counties. Rain shower are still likely \nalong and south of I-94, with chances then diminishing as one heads \nnorth. There is still some potential for 500 MB heights to close \noff, which would enhance showers and allow for a slower progression \ntonight. However, vast majority of solutions still highlight well \nunder 1 inch of rain.  \n\nThe second wave coming out of the central Plains looks to be the \nstronger one now, moving through southeast Michigan during the \nfavorable Sunday afternoon period. This should assure a greater \ncoverage of showers across southeast Michigan, as associated surface \nreflection moves right through the middle section of the CWA.\nWith MLcapes aoa 1000 J/kg and 700 MB dew pts around 8 C, torrential \nrain causing localized urban and low-lying flooding is a concern. \nHowever, activity looks progressive enough. Also, weak mid level \nlapse rates and 0-6 KM bulk shear values under 30 knots look\nto limit severe chances. \n\nDry weather returns for Monday and Tuesday as large a high pressure \nover the western Ohio Valley extends into southeast Michigan \nunderneath west-northwest upper level confluent flow. \n\nA large upper level low/trough centered over Hudson Bay will direct \na strong Pacific shortwave and strengthening surface low through the \ncentral Great Lakes on Wednesday, per 00z Euro. Cool Canadian air \nwill follow, with 850 MB temps bottoming out around 6 C by Friday.",
